rity | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 — Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing parity data and go/no-go scope | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n| HLR-2 — Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n| HLR-4 — Documentation of supported AMD hardware, drivers, validated models | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 |\n\nHLR-3 maps to two epics because it spans two components owned by the same team\n(Training Operator code support vs. Kueue quota configuration) — Rule 1 requires\nthe component boundary split; the quota coupling between them is an AC gate on\nthe training epic, not an edge.\n\n**docs-authoring priority exception**: E005 covers HLR-4 (P2) but, per the\ndocs-authoring priority exception, derives its priority from the strategy\nfrontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. It does not push priority upstream.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n- **KServe (passive)**: KServe requires no code changes for ROCm enablement —\n  the gap is the runtime image, not the serving lifecycle layer. Per the\n  active/passive rule, KServe compatibility is validated as acceptance criteria\n  on E002 (the nearest active component's epic, same owning team RHOAI Serving):\n  an InferenceService deploying the ROCm vLLM runtime reaches Ready.\n- **Distributed Workloads**: the strategy's HLR-3 \"amd.com/gpu quota handling\"\n  is the Kueue quota/admission surface, which is the Distributed Workloads\n  component — distinct from the Training Operator component handling PyTorchJob\n  execution. Both are owned by the Distributed Workloads team.\n- **Documentation**: HLR-4 introduces documentation work; the canonical\n  component `Documentation` is used. Owning team assigned to RHOAI Serving (the\n  strategy provides no Impacted Teams table; see Health Warnings).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional\n  Requirements, and no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Team\n  assignments were derived from the architecture-context owning teams (KServe and\n  Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n  Distributed Workloads). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR\n  text.\n- **Hardware access (Open Question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes are an\n  availability precondition controlled by procurement/partner-lab parties. It is\n  recorded as a risk on E001 (whose work includes securing access), not as an\n  acceptance criterion or a standalone epic — the campaign's deliverable is the\n  parity report, which is what its ACs sit on.\n- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (Open Question 2)**: AMD's runtime image release\n  cadence is externally controlled and may not align with platform release\n  trains. Recorded as a risk on E002 (pinned/rebuilt base images); it affects\n  timing, not which epics exist.\n- **Shared hardware coordination**: E001 (serving validation) and E003/E004\n  (training validation) will contend for the same scarce AMD Instinct nodes.\n  This is a scheduling/coordination risk, deliberately not modeled as a DAG\n  dependency.\n- **Priority inversion note (informational)**: HLR-4 is P2 while its epic E005\n  is P1 — this is the mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n  priority Major → P1), not an inconsistency in inheritance.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe upstream ROCm image cadence (and hardware availability) is an external\ndependency with uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value / risk reduction)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go\n  scope — the strategy's core risk-reduction deliverable), E003 and E004