training] --> E005\n38\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | Step 5 Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go output determines which models the productized ROCm runtime supports — the productization epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate recorded as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002. The outcome is a parameterized model list (same epic shape regardless of which subset clears), so no conditional branch epics are generated; if no model clears, E002 is removed per its fallback. |\n46\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | The campaign report is the authoritative source of the validated model list that the documentation must state; docs are authored against this artifact. |\n47\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built, including the productized runtime and its supported model list. |\n48\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation covers ROCm training support and prerequisites delivered by this work. |\n49\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation reflects the quota model in effect for AMD GPU workloads. |\n50\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 13 (configuration reference → AC gate) | The amd.com/gpu quota configuration is a configuration reference for training validation, not an API/CRD/library the Training Operator code builds against. Coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E003 (jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota), never as a DAG edge. |\n51\t| E001/E002 ∥ E003/E004 (no edges) | Rule 14 (independent repos/work streams) | Serving enablement (vLLM runtime) and training enablement (Training Operator, Kueue config) live in different components/repos with no shared artifacts; the shared need for AMD hardware is a coordination risk (see Health Warnings), not a dependency. |\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n56\t|-----|----------|---------|\n57\t| HLR-1 — Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing parity data and go/no-go scope | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n58\t| HLR-2 — Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n59\t| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n60\t| HLR-4 — Documentation of supported AMD hardware, drivers, validated models | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 |\n61\t\n62\tHLR-3 maps to two epics because it spans two components owned by the same team\n63\t(Training Operator code support vs. Kueue quota configuration) — Rule 1 requires\n64\tthe component boundary split; the quota coupling between them is an AC gate on\n65\tthe training epic, not an edge.\n66\t\n67\t**docs-authoring priority exception**: E005 covers HLR-4 (P2) but, per the\n68\tdocs-authoring priority exception, derives its priority from the strategy\n69\tfrontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. It does not push priority upstream.\n70\t\n71\t## Component Mapping Notes\n72\t\n73\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n74\t- **KServe (passive)**: KServe requires no code changes for ROCm enablement —\n75\t  the gap is the runtime image, not the serving lifecycle layer. Per the\n76\t  active/passive rule, KServe compatibility is validated as acceptance criteria\n77\t  on E002 (the nearest active component's epic, same owning team RHOAI Serving):\n78\t  an InferenceService deploying the ROCm vLLM runtime reaches Ready.\n79\t- **Distributed Workloads**: the strategy's HLR-3 \"amd.com/gpu quota handling\"\n80\t  is the Kueue quota/admission surface, which is the Distributed Workloads\n81\t  component — distinct from the Training Operator component handling PyTorchJob\n82\t  execution. Both are owned by the Distributed Workloads team.\n83\t- **Documentation**: HLR-4 introduces documentation work; the canonical\n84\t  component `Documentation` is used. Owning team assigned to RHOAI Serving (the\n85\t  strategy provides no Impacted Teams table; see Health Warnings).\n86\t\n87\t## Health Warnings\n88\t\n89\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n90\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional\n91\t  Requirements, and no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Team\n92\t  assignments were derived from the architecture-context owning teams (KServe and\n93\t  Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n94\t  Distributed Workloads). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR\n95\t  text.\n96\t- **Hardware access (Open Question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes are an\n97\t  availability precondition controlled by procurement/partner-lab parties. It is\n98\t  recorded as a risk on E001 (whose work includes securing access), not as an\n99\t  acceptance criterion or a standalone epic — the campaign's deliverable is the\n100\t  parity report, which is what its ACs sit on.\n101\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (Open Question 2)**: AMD's runtime image release\n102\t  cadence is externally controlled and may not align with platform release\n103\t  trains. Recorded as a risk on E002 (pinned/rebuilt base images); it affects\n104\t  timing, not which epics exist.\n105\t- **Shared hardware coordination**: E001 (serving validation) and E003/E004\n106\t  (training validation) will contend for the same scarce AMD Instinct nodes.\n107\t  This is a scheduling/coordination risk, deliberately not modeled as a DAG\n108\t  dependency.\n109\t- **Priority inversion note (informational)**: HLR-4 is P2 while its epic E005\n110\t  is P1 — this is the mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n111\t  priority Major → P1), not an inconsistency in inheritance.\n112\t\n113\t## Tiered Delivery\n114\t\n115\tThe upstream ROCm image cadence (and hardware availability) is an external\n116\tdependency with uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n117\t\n118\t- **Tier 1 (independent value / risk reduction)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go\n119\t  scope — the strategy's core risk-reduction deliverable), E003 and E004\n120\t  (training enablement and quota handling, usable on AMD GPU nodes independent\n121\t  of the serving productization decision).\n122\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 (productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime) is gated by\n123\t  the campaign outcome and by upstream image availability; E005 follows all\n124\t  implementation work.\n125\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bfd67e549c9fa30","agent_id":"afc313d1924b62d5e","session_id":"8431d8a2-78f2-40cd-ac08-9c04fa95fff4","uuid":"ead8a2d8-98dd-4dea-8323-d64919ba6fa0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:24:36.708Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbdb749d746da6df","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
