{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b954a2201421948","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\t**Strategy**: AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training\n12\t\n13\t**Triage outcome**: Not triaged. The strategy spans four components and two\n14\towning teams (RHOAI Serving, Distributed Workloads), contains a P0 unknown\n15\t(no first-hand ROCm parity data) whose resolution determines productization\n16\tscope, and includes hardware-dependent validation plus code/config plus\n17\tdocumentation work — neither Step 0 escape from full decomposition applies.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving parity and go/no-go scope | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD hardware support, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 Productize vLLM ROCm runtime]\n34\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Documentation]\n35\t    E002 --> E005\n36\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n37\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E002 | Step 5 Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go output determines which models the productized ROCm runtime supports — the productization epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate recorded as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002. The outcome is a parameterized model list (same epic shape regardless of which subset clears), so no conditional branch epics are generated; if no model clears, E002 is removed per its fallback. |\n45\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | The campaign report is the authoritative source of the validated model list that the documentation must state; docs are authored against this artifact. |\n46\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built, including the productized runtime and its supported model list. |\n47\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation covers ROCm training support and prerequisites delivered by this work. |\n48\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation reflects the quota model in effect for AMD GPU workloads. |\n49\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 13 (configuration reference → AC gate) | The amd.com/gpu quota configuration is a configuration reference for training validation, not an API/CRD/library the Training Operator code builds against. Coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E003 (jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota), never as a DAG edge. |\n50\t| E001/E002 ∥ E003/E004 (no edges) | Rule 14 (independent repos/work streams) | Serving enablement (vLLM runtime) and training enablement (Training Operator, Kueue config) live in different components/repos with no shared artifacts; the shared need for AMD hardware is a coordination risk (see Health Warnings), not a dependency. |\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n55\t|-----|----------|---------|\n56\t| HLR-1 — Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing parity data and go/no-go scope | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n57\t| HLR-2 — Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n58\t| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n59\t| HLR-4 — Documentation of supported AMD hardware, drivers, validated models | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 |\n60\t\n61\tHLR-3 maps to two epics because it spans two components owned by the same team\n62\t(Training Operator code support vs. Kueue quota configuration) — Rule 1 requires\n63\tthe component boundary split; the quota coupling between them is an AC gate on\n64\tthe training epic, not an edge.\n65\t\n66\t**docs-authoring priority exception**: E005 covers HLR-4 (P2) but, per the\n67\tdocs-authoring priority exception, derives its priority from the strategy\n68\tfrontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. It does not push priority upstream.\n69\t\n70\t## Component Mapping Notes\n71\t\n72\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n73\t- **KServe (passive)**: KServe requires no code changes for ROCm enablement —\n74\t  the gap is the runtime image, not the serving lifecycle layer. Per the\n75\t  active/passive rule, KServe compatibility is validated as acceptance criteria\n76\t  on E002 (the nearest active component's epic, same owning team RHOAI Serving):\n77\t  an InferenceService deploying the ROCm vLLM runtime reaches Ready.\n78\t- **Distributed Workloads**: the strategy's HLR-3 \"amd.com/gpu quota handling\"\n79\t  is the Kueue quota/admission surface, which is the Distributed Workloads\n80\t  component — distinct from the Training Operator component handling PyTorchJob\n81\t  execution. Both are owned by the Distributed Workloads team.\n82\t- **Documentation**: HLR-4 introduces documentation work; the canonical\n83\t  component `Documentation` is used. Owning team assigned to RHOAI Serving (the\n84\t  strategy provides no Impacted Teams table; see Health Warnings).\n85\t\n86\t## Health Warnings\n87\t\n88\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n89\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional\n90\t  Requirements, and no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Team\n91\t  assignments were derived from the architecture-context owning teams (KServe and\n92\t  Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads →\n93\t  Distributed Workloads). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR\n94\t  text.\n95\t- **Hardware access (Open Question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes are an\n96\t  availability precondition controlled by procurement/partner-lab parties. It is\n97\t  recorded as a risk on E001 (whose work includes securing access), not as an\n98\t  acceptance criterion or a standalone epic — the campaign's deliverable is the\n99\t  parity report, which is what its ACs sit on.\n100\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (Open Question 2)**: AMD's runtime image release\n101\t  cadence is externally controlled and may not align with platform release\n102\t  trains. Recorded as a risk on E002 (pinned/rebuilt base images); it affects\n103\t  timing, not which epics exist.\n104\t- **Shared hardware coordination**: E001 (serving validation) and E003/E004\n105\t  (training validation) will contend for the same scarce AMD Instinct nodes.\n106\t  This is a scheduling/coordination risk, deliberately not modeled as a DAG\n107\t  dependency.\n108\t- **Priority inversion note (informational)**: HLR-4 is P2 while its epic E005\n109\t  is P1 — this is the mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n110\t  priority Major → P1), not an inconsistency in inheritance.\n111\t\n112\t## Tiered Delivery\n113\t\n114\tThe upstream ROCm image cadence (and hardware availability) is an external\n115\tdependency with uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n116\t\n117\t- **Tier 1 (independent value / risk reduction)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go\n118\t  scope — the strategy's core risk-reduction deliverable), E003 and E004\n119\t  (training enablement and quota handling, usable on AMD GPU nodes independent\n120\t  of the serving productization decision).\n121\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 (productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime) is gated by\n122\t  the campaign outcome and by upstream image availability; E005 follows all\n123\t  implementation work.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96d7f0e5116e17af","agent_id":"ac7051d126c0997ab","session_id":"8431d8a2-78f2-40cd-ac08-9c04fa95fff4","uuid":"c73de361-ca3b-46c1-afdd-3e5f817d5408","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:30:15.805Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b954a2201421948","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
